  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nMid-level ridging remains in place across the Upper Ohio \nValley this afternoon, leading to plenty of sunshine and above-\nnormal temperatures, with many locations expected to reach or \nexceed 80 degrees for maximums.\n\nAlso today, a Central Plans closed low begins to degrade with\ntime, eventually ejecting as a shortwave trough across the Ohio\nVally through Friday. This will help to push a cold front across\nthe region tomorrow, with rain chances increasing ahead of it\nstarting after midnight to the northwest of Pittsburgh, with the\nmain period of rain during the daylight hours and into the early\nevening.\n\nSouthwest flow ahead of the boundary will promote some\nmoistening of the column, with precipitable water values\nincreasing to around 1.7 inches immediately ahead of the\nboundary, which represents top-end climatology. Even so, the\noverall weak forcing and unfavorable frontal timing will work\nagainst a threat of heavy rain. HREF probabilities of 24-hour \nrain totals exceeding 0.50 inch are less than 10 percent over \nmost of our region, with only a few bands of 20 to 40 percent\npotential. Plus, while 0-6km shear should range from 40 to 50\nknots during FROPA, SBCAPE will likely be limited to around 200\nJ/kg or less. What instability there is is packed close to the\nsurface, with most model soundings suggesting updrafts will top\nout below -10C, limiting any lightning potential. So, it is\nappropriate that the SPC convective outlook for Friday does not\neven include our area in a risk for \"general\" thunderstorms.\n\nMost rain will end Friday evening with the departure of the\nfront, although a few showers may linger in the ridges southeast\nof Pittsburgh into Saturday morning.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nModel ensembles indicate broad upper troughing should persist \nacross the NE CONUS from the weekend into the middle of next \nweek. A southern stream shortwave could bring a few showers to \nareas south and east of Pittsburgh on Sunday. This disturbance would\nbe followed by an influx of cooler air into the region, dropping\ntemperatures below climatology early next week while another run\nof dry weather begins thanks to high pressure.\n\nOne thing to watch will be the potential for frost both Tuesday and\nWednesday mornings to the northeast of Pittsburgh, as well as \nin the usual Laurel Highland/northern West Virginia cold spots. \nThe NBM paints a 35 to 75 percent chance of low temperatures \ncolder than 37 degrees north of US-422 both mornings - which \nwould be chilly enough for at least patchy frost given clear and\ncalm overnight conditions. Temperatures from Wednesday on \nshould build back towards more seasonal values.",
